  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250054/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
